(Everything has a subjective reason. Progress is acting as a mighty) Tj T*
(dynamic force in changing men?s viewpoint of life and things. Suppose) Tj T*
(the stroke oar on a varsity crew, while in a race against an opposing) Tj T*
(crew from a competitive institution, should suddenly stop rowing in) Tj T*
(harmony with his associates and begin to row backwards-that crew) Tj T*
(would not get very far without trouble. Suppose a lawn mower should be) Tj T*
(reversed and forced to run backwards--there would not be much progress) Tj T*
(made in cutting grass on that lawn. Varsity crews and lawn mowers must) Tj T*
(move forward. Business men must advance with the times.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A great merchant in Chicago tells a good story of his youth. He was a) Tj T*
(member of a state regiment of militia. On a certain occasion, his) Tj T*
(company was sent out on dress parade. An old maiden aunt, with) Tj T*
(considerable colonial blood in her veins, took much pride in her) Tj T*
(nephew and his company. While reviewing the parade, she was suddenly) Tj T*
(heard to exclaim: ?Why, every single man in that company is out of) Tj T*
(step excepting my nephew.? Most men who fail to get on in the world do) Tj T*
(not realize that success lies in keeping step--in making progress with) Tj T*
(changing conditions. They generally make the mistake of thinking that) Tj T*
(the world and everything in it is out of harmony with themselves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: New ideas worth searching for]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A business man of successful experience realizes that ideas--newer and) Tj T*
(better principles of conducting business--are of the greatest value,) Tj T*
(and he also knows that it pays him to search for them. The same old) Tj T*
(way of doing things cannot longer be successfully employed month after) Tj T*
(month and year after year as under the old regime. The business man) Tj T*
(must be modern, up-to-date. The physician or lawyer finds that to) Tj T*
(compete successfully he is compelled to search without ceasing in) Tj T*
(order that he may comprehend the advancement in treatments or) Tj T*
(procedures. ?To the man who fails belong the excuses.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Demand for trained men]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(President James, of the University of Illinois, was asked if there was) Tj T*
(any demand from business houses for college-bred men. His reply was:) Tj T*
(?The demand has been far in excess of the supply since courses in) Tj T*
(business administration were established in our institution seven) Tj T*
(years ago. Each year has brought many more requests than we have men) Tj T*
(to recommend.? Ten years ago President James would have been ridiculed) Tj T*
(for advancing this new idea for the establishment of a school of) Tj T*
(commerce in connection with a university. Today, commercial schools) Tj T*
(are a part of the regularly established courses of nearly all of the) Tj T*
(great universities of our country. Men trained in the theory, practice) Tj T*
(and administration of business will always occupy the best positions) Tj T*
(and will always command the greatest salaries.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Value of new ideas in business emergencies]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
