() Tj T*
(The last of these theories regards a crisis as essentially due to a) Tj T*
(failure of credit. It is seen that a large part of modern industry is) Tj T*
(carried on by borrowed capital, by roundabout processes and for a) Tj T*
(distant market and not upon order. That is, the success of a business) Tj T*
(depends upon its ability to sell its goods when produced. Now the) Tj T*
(aggregate volume of transactions that can be carried on in a year, so) Tj T*
(runs the theory, depends upon the efficiency of the credit system;) Tj T*
(that is, in general, upon the freedom with which banks are willing to) Tj T*
(loan money to people who engage to repay it in the future out of their) Tj T*
(ventures. If for any reason the banks reduce this accommodation the) Tj T*
(amount of business that can be transacted upon borrowed capital is) Tj T*
(lessened. Either some transactions must stop or prices must fall.) Tj T*
(Either of these events causes commercial disaster. The contraction of) Tj T*
(credit makes it impossible to get the goods into the right hands, and) Tj T*
(so we have the phenomena of over-production in a great many lines. As) Tj T*
(exchange and transportation have developed and markets widened, crises) Tj T*
(have become more universal. According to this theory, they are) Tj T*
(inseparably connected with the use of credit and can be controlled) Tj T*
(only by a more careful granting of credit by the banks to industrial) Tj T*
(managers. Another phase of the credit theory is presented by those who) Tj T*
(insist that the cause of crises is the rhythmic overestimation of the) Tj T*
(profits to be secured out of certain lines of production, or their) Tj T*
(over-capitalization. The new enterprises are financed by the banks on) Tj T*
(the basis of this mistaken over-capitalization, their organizers) Tj T*
(engage to pay rates of interest which they cannot earn, and the crash) Tj T*
(inevitably follows. This is often called the over-capitalization) Tj T*
(theory, and is essentially psychological in its character.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is no doubt as to the truth contained in this last theory. It) Tj T*
(helps to explain the rhythmic periodicity of crises. After every          60) Tj T*
(period of business depression confidence revives and hope is renewed;) Tj T*
(overestimation of the success of new ventures is inevitable. Then) Tj T*
(follows a mistaken investment of capital in certain lines of) Tj T*
(production, as in railroads in 1884, and a relative over-production at) Tj T*
(profitable prices of certain commodities. The true explanation seems) Tj T*
(to be found in a combination of the over-production and) Tj T*
(over-capitalization theories.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The practical problem that presents itself in this connection is the) Tj T*
(question as to whether it is possible to prevent the recurrence of) Tj T*
(crises. In view of the explanation just given it would seem that they) Tj T*
(must be regarded as unpreventable as long as industry is carried on) Tj T*
(under the competitive capitalistic system of production and the modern) Tj T*
(credit system. Moreover, crops differ in amount from year to year and) Tj T*
(probably always will. Human production and human genius are unequal.) Tj T*
