(VIII. LABOR ORGANIZATIONS AND COLLECTIVE BARGAINING.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As modern capitalistic production caused the growth of a distinct) Tj T*
(wage-earning class and brought about a sharp separation between) Tj T*
(employers and laborers, and as the latter were thrown upon their own      69) Tj T*
(resources under the prevailing theories of free competition and free) Tj T*
(contract, it was inevitable that they should organize to secure their) Tj T*
(interests as a class. The growth of labor organizations has been) Tj T*
(greatest in those countries where the laborer has been forced to) Tj T*
(depend mostly upon his own efforts for protection and improvement,) Tj T*
(namely, in England and the United States. On the continent of Europe,) Tj T*
(on the other hand, where the individual has been accustomed to look to) Tj T*
(the government for the redress of industrial grievances, there has) Tj T*
(been a much less vigorous and spontaneous development of such) Tj T*
(organizations. They are a product of the nineteenth century and had) Tj T*
(their origin in modern machine production.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The growth of labor organizations in the United States has proceeded) Tj T*
(hand in hand with the industrial development of this country, and has) Tj T*
(been especially rapid since the Civil War. Two distinct types of trade) Tj T*
(unions may be noted--the local and the national \(or international\)) Tj T*
(unions. The former, which comprises members who live and work in the) Tj T*
(same locality, is the primary unit, and dates back to the beginning of) Tj T*
(the century. Each local union, even when subordinate to a national) Tj T*
(organization, is a self-governing unit, and is absolutely democratic.) Tj T*
(Its relation to the national body has been well compared to that of) Tj T*
(one of our states to the United States. The first national union was) Tj T*
(not formed until 1850, but now these far surpass the locals in) Tj T*
(importance. Their government is representative, as they are made up of) Tj T*
(local unions. The great majority of the national trade unions are) Tj T*
(bound together in the powerful federal organization, the American) Tj T*
(Federation of Labor. The membership of this body numbers considerably) Tj T*
(over 1,000,000, while the railroad unions, which are not connected) Tj T*
(with it, claim about 125,000 more. Probably not far from 1,500,000) Tj T*
(persons in the United States belong to labor organizations, which is) Tj T*
(about 10 per cent of the total working population or about 15 per         70) Tj T*
(cent of those engaged in trade and transportation, manufacturing and) Tj T*
(mechanical pursuits. While this does not seem a very large proportion) Tj T*
(and is not as large as the membership of British trade unions, yet it) Tj T*
(must be remembered that they constitute on the whole the elite of the) Tj T*
(labor world and exercise an authority and power out of proportion to) Tj T*
(their numbers. Many other workmen, who do not themselves belong to the) Tj T*
