(the fundamental principle of our modern wage system is freedom of) Tj T*
(contract. This is guaranteed in our federal and state constitutions       88) Tj T*
(as both a personal and a property right. As a result of this fact the) Tj T*
(courts have generally declared unconstitutional any legislation,) Tj T*
(designed to protect the interests of labor, that seemed to abrogate) Tj T*
(this freedom of contract or that savored of class legislation. Efforts) Tj T*
(to improve the condition of labor by legislation have therefore met) Tj T*
(with especial obstacles in this country. On the whole, however, means) Tj T*
(have been discovered of evading these constitutional restrictions when) Tj T*
(it has seemed clearly demanded by the welfare of society, and the) Tj T*
(history of labor legislation in this country is one of fairly steady) Tj T*
(progress. The early laws were practically confined to imprisonment for) Tj T*
(debt, mechanics? liens, the hours of education of children employed in) Tj T*
(factories, and similar matters. Nothing noteworthy was accomplished) Tj T*
(until 1866 when Massachusetts passed an eight-hour child labor law for) Tj T*
(children under fourteen; in 1874 she passed a ten-hour law for women) Tj T*
(and children under eighteen, engaged in manufacturing establishments,) Tj T*
(and in 1877 enacted the first factory inspection act, which has since) Tj T*
(been copied in about twenty-four states, and without which mere) Tj T*
(legislation is of little avail.) Tj T*
