(The original legal doctrine regarding liability for accident in) Tj T*
(England and America, which is still practically unmodified in the) Tj T*
(latter country, was based on the principle of individual) Tj T*
(responsibility for acts of negligence. Briefly stated the common law) Tj T*
(doctrine is that an employer must provide reasonably safe conditions) Tj T*
(of employment, and that then the employe assumes the risks incident to) Tj T*
(the occupation, or arising from the carelessness of fellow-servants;) Tj T*
(moreover, even if the employer has been remiss, the employe cannot) Tj T*
(collect damages if he has been guilty of contributory negligence.) Tj T*
(These three doctrines--assumption of risk, doctrine of the) Tj T*
(fellow-servant, and contributory negligence--have been used) Tj T*
(practically to free the employer from all responsibility in cases) Tj T*
(where injured employes have sought to secure damages. Moreover, as) Tj T*
(has been shown above, many cases exist where it is impossible to fix      99) Tj T*
(the blame on either employer, employe, or a third party, and in such) Tj T*
(cases no compensation could be secured for injury under the law. The) Tj T*
(full rigor of the common law, which has worked out so unfairly for the) Tj T*
(workingman in modern machine production, has been modified in about) Tj T*
(twenty-seven states by statutes defining more exactly the duties of) Tj T*
(the employer, and repealing the fellow-servant doctrine in regard to) Tj T*
(railway employes and in a few states in regard to all mechanical) Tj T*
(industries. With these exceptions, however, the law of employers?) Tj T*
(liability has not been changed, and compensation for industrial) Tj T*
(accidents must be sought by injured employes through a suit for) Tj T*
(damages against the employer. In 1906 and again in 1908 Congress) Tj T*
(passed a federal employers? liability act, limited to common carriers,) Tj T*
(which, however, represents only development along the lines of) Tj T*
(negligence law. That is, we are still proceeding upon the assumption) Tj T*
(that in every accident which occurs somebody is to blame. We shall) Tj T*
(have to look to foreign countries for a practical application of the) Tj T*
(principle that the cost of accidents in modern industry should be made) Tj T*
(a charge upon the industry itself, and ultimately be incorporated in) Tj T*
(the higher price of the article produced.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Germany was the first country to introduce the principle of compulsory) Tj T*
(accident insurance in 1884. Employers are there organized into) Tj T*
(associations and sections and are compelled to bear the expense of) Tj T*
(granting to injured workingmen compensation, which amounts to about) Tj T*
(two-thirds their average wages. England in 1897, by the passage of the) Tj T*
(Workmen?s Compensation Act, adopted the principle ?that a workman is) Tj T*
(entitled for all accidents of occupation to a moderate and reasonable) Tj T*
(compensation.? Twenty-three countries, or practically all the advanced) Tj T*
(industrial nations of the world except the United States, have passed) Tj T*
(laws to compensate sufferers for all accidents of industry, thus         100) Tj T*
(placing the burden of industrial accidents upon the industry as such) Tj T*
(and not upon the laborer.) Tj T*
