(of the causes of poverty pointed out. It will be sufficient here to) Tj T*
(try to answer the question which has often been asked: Are the rich) Tj T*
(growing richer and the poor poorer? Though the first part of the) Tj T*
(question has just been affirmed, the second part may be denied. The) Tj T*
(nineteenth century has witnessed a vast improvement in the condition) Tj T*
(of the laboring man, who has shared in the increasing wealth which he) Tj T*
(has helped to produce. Wages have steadily increased, the hours of) Tj T*
(labor have been reduced, and the material well-being of the) Tj T*
(wage-earner is greater today than it has ever been before. It has more) Tj T*
(than once been pointed out by writers on this subject that with an) Tj T*
(equal distribution of wealth no one would be well-to-do, while many) Tj T*
(others insist that inequality in itself is a desirable thing. Greater) Tj T*
(diffusion of wealth can come about only by very slow processes, and) Tj T*
(permanent plenty can be secured only by a great increase in the) Tj T*
(accumulations of capital and the efficiency of each worker. Any) Tj T*
(suggested reform, therefore, that would weaken the motives to thrift) Tj T*
(and industry must be rejected.) Tj T*
