(increases a smaller percentage is spent for food and a larger            129) Tj T*
(percentage for education, health, recreation, etc.; while the) Tj T*
(percentage spent for clothing, rent, fuel and light remains) Tj T*
(approximately the same. A higher civilization and culture for the mass) Tj T*
(of the people can only be secured by expanding the group of culture) Tj T*
(expenditures. As long as these remain unsatisfied for the ordinary) Tj T*
(family we cannot claim to have attained our economic goal. The author) Tj T*
(of a recent study of conditions in New York City, where the cost of) Tj T*
(living is high, concludes that a ?fair living wage for a workingman?s) Tj T*
(family in New York City should be at least $728 a year, or a steady) Tj T*
(income of $14 a week.?[44] The actual earnings are certainly below) Tj T*
(this figure.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One of the problems which has often proved very puzzling is the) Tj T*
(relation between saving and spending. At what point should one stop) Tj T*
(spending in order to save? If the satisfaction of our wants is the) Tj T*
(object of production, why should we save at all? This is the point) Tj T*
(urged by the author of a specious little book called ?The Fallacy of) Tj T*
(Saving.? The problem can be most easily solved by a more careful) Tj T*
(analysis of terms. In the popular view, saving involves the withdrawal) Tj T*
(of goods or money from use, while spending means putting them to) Tj T*
(immediate use. The spendthrift is proverbially popular. ?If the rich) Tj T*
(do not spend, the poor die of hunger,? said Montesquieu. Saving may) Tj T*
(take the form of hoarding or withdrawing things from use, but nowadays) Tj T*
(this is practised only by misers; saving ordinarily takes the form of) Tj T*
(investment in some productive enterprise, either directly or through a) Tj T*
(bank. In this way a demand is created for goods just as truly as) Tj T*
(though the money had been spent for a dinner or a suit of clothes.) Tj T*
(Saving is spending, but it is spending for the future rather than the) Tj T*
(present; it usually causes the production of permanent material goods) Tj T*
(rather than transient or immaterial pleasures. Another cause of the) Tj T*
