(Now, inasmuch as the prices of all goods and services are measured and) Tj T*
(expressed in terms of money, it is clear that a fall in the value of) Tj T*
(money means a rise of general prices; the value of each commodity is) Tj T*
(now expressed in terms of a larger number of less valuable units or) Tj T*
(dollars. Prices will be high if the quantity of money in circulation) Tj T*
(in a country is large; they will be low if the quantity is small. To) Tj T*
(the question, which is better for a country, high prices or low) Tj T*
(prices, it may be answered that it is a matter of indifference,) Tj T*
(provided only that there is enough money to do the work of exchange) Tj T*
(efficiently and that fluctuations are prevented. Just how much) Tj T*
(constitutes enough is, however, a matter of contention. In the) Tj T*
(undeveloped sections of our country, where capital is scarce and) Tj T*
(banking facilities undeveloped and where most of the people are) Tj T*
(debtors, there has always been a demand for cheap and abundant money.) Tj T*
(Capital and money have been confused and the need of one has led to a) Tj T*
(demand for the other.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is not a matter of indifference, however, whether prices be rising) Tj T*
(or falling, that is, whether inflation or contraction of the currency) Tj T*
(is taking place. A period of falling prices means hardship and) Tj T*
(injustice to debtors and producers of goods, as farmers,) Tj T*
(manufacturers, etc. Having contracted obligations and engaged in the     139) Tj T*
(production of commodities with the expectation of a given price, they) Tj T*
(find their goods worth less when ready for the market and themselves) Tj T*
(confronted with a loss instead of the anticipated profit. Under such) Tj T*
(circumstances a contraction of the currency and falling prices means) Tj T*
(lessened production of wealth. Consequently many writers, and even so) Tj T*
(good an economist as President Walker, have urged that a slow steady) Tj T*
(inflation of the currency would promote trade and ?give a fillip to) Tj T*
(industry.? The monetary history of the United States is filled with) Tj T*
(attempts to realize this in practice: colonial and revolutionary bills) Tj T*
(of credit were first issued; when these were forbidden by the new) Tj T*
(Constitution resort was had to issues by state banks. When the Federal) Tj T*
(Government began the issue of greenbacks and restricted the use of) Tj T*
(state bank notes, the inflationists looked to this source for) Tj T*
(assistance. After the defeat of the Greenback party, they turned) Tj T*
(finally to the coinage of silver, which was now falling in price, and) Tj T*
(the question of bimetallism in the United States was made a practical) Tj T*
(political issue.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Down to 1870 practically all the nations of Europe and America had the) Tj T*
(system of bimetallism at ratios of 15Ω or 16 to 1. About that date) Tj T*
(the great increase in the supply of gold and the fall in the value of) Tj T*
(silver led one country after another to abandon the latter and to) Tj T*
(adopt the system of gold monometallism. This was vigorously resisted) Tj T*
(by many persons and several fruitless efforts made to secure a system) Tj T*
(of international bimetallism. Failing that, the friends of silver in) Tj T*
(this country endeavored to secure independent action by the United) Tj T*
(States alone, and were ultimately successful in obtaining the purchase) Tj T*
ET
