(The arguments in favor of bimetallism are as various as the motives of) Tj T*
(its advocates, but two or three of the more important ones may be        140) Tj T*
(briefly stated. It is urged because it would give a more stable) Tj T*
(measure of value than either silver or gold alone could do; and the) Tj T*
(evil effects of fluctuations in the value of gold since 1873 are) Tj T*
(pointed out to illustrate this contention. Monometallists answer this) Tj T*
(by asserting that most of the price changes can be accounted for by) Tj T*
(improvements in production; that even if they were caused by a) Tj T*
(contraction of the currency, this was simply one of the risks of) Tj T*
(business; and finally, that the evil effects of falling prices are) Tj T*
(offset by a corresponding reduction in interest rates. A second) Tj T*
(argument of the bimetallists was the alleged insufficiency of gold on) Tj T*
(which to do the world?s business. As this has been practically met by) Tj T*
(the phenomenal increase in gold production in the last decade,) Tj T*
(especially since the gold discoveries in Alaska, it is not necessary) Tj T*
(to dwell upon this argument. On February 1, 1909, the per capita) Tj T*
(circulation of money in the United States reached $35.00, the highest) Tj T*
(point in our history. A final argument of the bimetallists concerns) Tj T*
(foreign trade: it would facilitate this by establishing a fixed) Tj T*
(par-of-exchange between all countries. While the weight of this may be) Tj T*
(admitted, it has been practically deprived of all force by the) Tj T*
(adoption of the gold standard by virtually all the industrially) Tj T*
(developed nations of the world. This last fact shows that the question) Tj T*
(has now been actually settled by the logic of events and today the) Tj T*
(issue of bimetallism has only an academic interest.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Another problem connected with money which has been removed from the) Tj T*
(arena of oratory to that of calm discussion is that of government) Tj T*
(paper money. It is urged, with much truth, that if a nation issued) Tj T*
(paper money instead of gold or silver, it would save all the expense) Tj T*
(of mining these metals. It would resemble, as Adam Smith said, the) Tj T*
