(Almost as important for the conduct of modern industry as machine) Tj T*
(methods and credit are the rapid means of transportation and) Tj T*
(communication furnished by our railroad, steamship, express, post) Tj T*
(office, telegraph and telephone systems. Indeed the development of) Tj T*
(industry on a national scale and its integration under centralized) Tj T*
(control has been made possible only by these improvements. But not) Tj T*
(only have these businesses rendered the centralization of industry) Tj T*
(possible; they themselves exhibit on a national scale concentration      146) Tj T*
(of control. They are all industries of increasing returns and lend) Tj T*
(themselves naturally to monopolistic control. At the very beginning of) Tj T*
(railroad construction one of the most far-sighted managers enunciated) Tj T*
(the doctrine that ?where combination is possible competition is) Tj T*
(impossible.? For years competition was regarded as the regulator of) Tj T*
(rates, pooling between railroads was forbidden, canals were advocated) Tj T*
(as competitors, and by every possible device it was sought to) Tj T*
(stimulate it. We are at last beginning to recognize the monopoly) Tj T*
(character of the railroad industry and to regulate it accordingly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Consolidation in the railroad world is not a new phenomenon nor is it) Tj T*
(confined to that industry, but it has proceeded further there than in) Tj T*
(any other line of business. The first form which combination took was) Tj T*
(that of pooling, according to which the traffic was ?pooled? and the) Tj T*
(earnings then divided among the companies entering into the pool) Tj T*
(according to some previous agreement. This was forbidden by the) Tj T*
(Interstate Commerce Act in 1887 and even more stringently by the) Tj T*
(Anti-Trust Act of 1890, and accordingly railroad managers next) Tj T*
(resorted to actual consolidation of competing lines. Where this has) Tj T*
(not been possible or desirable, virtual combination has been secured) Tj T*
(by the so-called ?community of interests? arrangements, based on the) Tj T*
(acquisition by one road of enough stock in competing lines to secure) Tj T*
(representation on their boards of directors. Today some eight or nine) Tj T*
(groups of capitalists control over two-thirds of the railway mileage) Tj T*
(of the United States, and according to a recent widely-published) Tj T*
(statement the late Mr. E. H. Harriman was credited with controlling,) Tj T*
(directly or indirectly, a system aggregating over 67,000 miles. These) Tj T*
(great consolidations have followed mainly the territorial groupings of) Tj T*
(railroads; the United States has now been districted out by a few) Tj T*
(large transportation companies, much as France, Italy, England and) Tj T*
(other European countries had previously been divided up. Consolidation   147) Tj T*
(has in many instances resulted in increased convenience to the public) Tj T*
(and in economies in management and operation, but it places a) Tj T*
(dangerous amount of power in the hands of a few men, which has not) Tj T*
(infrequently been abused, and should clearly be under strict) Tj T*
(government control.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The primary economic problem connected with railways is always the) Tj T*
(question of rates. This has been called in a recent book ?the heart of) Tj T*
(the railroad problem.? The first fact that strikes the student of the) Tj T*
(subject is the great reduction in rates and fares in the past) Tj T*
(twenty-five years, especially in freight rates. From 1.24 cents in) Tj T*
(1882 the average revenue per ton mile received by railroads in the) Tj T*
(United States has decreased to .748 cents in 1906. Freight rates,) Tj T*
