BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(especially through rates for bulky traffic, are considerably lower in) Tj T*
(this country, and passenger fares somewhat higher, than in Europe. But) Tj T*
(the vital problem connected with rates is not as to their relative) Tj T*
(cheapness or extortionateness; it concerns rather the granting of) Tj T*
(discriminating rates. Discriminations may be of three kinds: those) Tj T*
(between different classes of goods, those between localities, and) Tj T*
(those between persons. The first group is based upon the) Tj T*
(classification of freight and rests upon differences in cost of) Tj T*
(shipment, in bulk, in risk, etc. If reasonably employed, this kind of) Tj T*
(discrimination is justifiable. Local discriminations, that is,) Tj T*
(charging different rates to different localities for substantially the) Tj T*
(same service, is not only unwarranted in most cases, but is) Tj T*
(short-sighted as well. Where superior facilities or especially keen) Tj T*
(competition exists, lower rates may be permitted for favored) Tj T*
(localities, but the arbitrary exercise of such powers by railway) Tj T*
(officials is thoroughly unjustifiable. Even less defensible is the) Tj T*
(practice, now happily less frequent, of granting discriminatory rates) Tj T*
(to favored individuals or corporations. They have been given by means    148) Tj T*
(of secret rates and rebates, by under-billing and under-classification,) Tj T*
(by free passes, etc. Both of these latter evils have been forbidden or) Tj T*
(greatly restricted by the passage of the Interstate Commerce Act in) Tj T*
(1887 and subsequent legislation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The public nature of railroads is now fairly well recognized in our) Tj T*
(law and is beginning to be understood by the people at large.) Tj T*
(Railroads enjoy peculiar privileges in the grant of corporate) Tj T*
(franchises and charters, in the right of eminent domain, and in) Tj T*
(enormous grants of land and money which have been made to them in this) Tj T*
(country. Moreover in the functions they perform the social character) Tj T*
(of their duties is emphasized, and they are under the necessity of) Tj T*
(maintaining a constant service open to all. Though they are owned by) Tj T*
(private investors and managed as private enterprises, they are) Tj T*
(essentially public enterprises as to their privileges, functions, and) Tj T*
(duties. Consequently most of the states have now undertaken, through) Tj T*
(commissions, to regulate the railroads in the public interest. Some) Tj T*
(thirty-one have appointed commissions, which probably control) Tj T*
(four-fifths of the traffic originating and ending in a single state.) Tj T*
(These state commissions differ in power, those of the Mississippi) Tj T*
(Valley and the South usually having mandatory powers, that is, power) Tj T*
(to prescribe and enforce maximum rates. In the eastern and central) Tj T*
(states commissions with supervisory powers merely, of investigation) Tj T*
(and report, have been created. The only exceptions are found in the) Tj T*
(Far West where the need of improved transportation facilities is more) Tj T*
(pressing than regulation, and in five eastern states whose) Tj T*
(legislatures are controlled by the railroad interests. While the state) Tj T*
(commissions have done and are doing valuable service, it is clear that) Tj T*
(the growth of giant railroad combinations which traverse several) Tj T*
(states necessitates federal control. The appointment of the Interstate) Tj T*
