(XVII. TAXATION AND TARIFF.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In no way does the State affect the interests of its citizens more) Tj T*
(vitally than in the sphere of taxation. The State in modern society is) Tj T*
(the people organized for certain collective purposes, as for the) Tj T*
(public defense, the preservation of domestic peace, and the) Tj T*
(furtherance of the social and industrial welfare. To carry out these) Tj T*
(objects money is needed and the State has therefore to collect from) Tj T*
(its citizens sufficient revenue to defray its expenditures. John Fiske) Tj T*
(has tersely defined taxes as ?portions of private property taken for) Tj T*
(public purposes.? Taxation thus implies a certain degree of) Tj T*
(compulsion; by it the Government interferes with the free choice of) Tj T*
(the individual and expends a part at least of his income for him in) Tj T*
(ways that he himself might not have chosen. The social and industrial) Tj T*
(consequences of a system of taxation may also be far-reaching and) Tj T*
(important. As Professor R. T. Ely says: ?Taxation may create) Tj T*
(monopolies, or it may prevent them; it may diffuse wealth, or it may) Tj T*
(control it; it may promote labor or equality of rights, or it may tend) Tj T*
(to the establishment of tyranny and despotism; it may be used to bring) Tj T*
(about reform, or it may be used to aggravate existing grievances and) Tj T*
(foster dissensions between classes.? It is evident therefore that the) Tj T*
(utmost care should be exercised in framing a system of taxation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Certain canons or rules of taxation were laid down by Adam Smith over) Tj T*
(a hundred years ago and have been generally endorsed by economists) Tj T*
(ever since. One was that taxes ought to be certain and not arbitrary,) Tj T*
(as to amount, time and manner of payment; another was that taxes ought) Tj T*
(to be levied in the manner most convenient to the tax-payer; and a) Tj T*
(third, that taxes ought to take as little as possible out of the) Tj T*
(pockets of the tax-payer over and above what is paid into the public     155) Tj T*
(treasury. These three maxims--certainty, convenience, and) Tj T*
(economy--have been generally accepted, but less general agreement) Tj T*
(exists in regard to the fourth, which states that the subjects of) Tj T*
(every state ought to contribute to the support of the Government as) Tj T*
(nearly as possible in proportion to their respective abilities. This) Tj T*
(rule has given rise to two problems: first, is ability the most just) Tj T*
(basis of taxation; and secondly, if so, how is ability to be measured?) Tj T*
(The theory of justice generally accepted by legal writers and by the) Tj T*
(American courts is expressed in the maxim that taxes should be) Tj T*
(proportioned to benefits received. The benefit theory affords a good) Tj T*
(rule in the assessment of local property taxes, but fails utterly in) Tj T*
(the domain of national and state affairs. Who can measure the benefits) Tj T*
(to each individual of an appropriation for a new war ship or for a) Tj T*
(state penitentiary or for the public school system? Probably the) Tj T*
(benefits are in inverse proportion to the income or wealth of the) Tj T*
(individual, and the heaviest taxes would then have to be apportioned) Tj T*
(to those least able to pay. Most economists today agree that taxes) Tj T*
