(should be apportioned according to ?faculty? or ability to pay. It) Tj T*
(satisfies better our sense of fairness and is more readily applicable) Tj T*
(than the benefit theory. In the last analysis, of course, it may be) Tj T*
(said that taxation in general must confer real benefits upon society) Tj T*
(or it will not be tolerated. Here, however, we are concerned with a) Tj T*
(rule of apportionment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The second practical problem encountered is when we attempt to apply) Tj T*
(the faculty principle in practice; how is ability to be measured?) Tj T*
(Three measures have been suggested: expenditure, income, and property.) Tj T*
(Expenditure is open to the objection that it would place an unduly) Tj T*
(large proportion of the tax burdens on the poor, whose expenditures) Tj T*
(are larger in proportion to their means than those of other classes of) Tj T*
(society. Property is objected to because large classes of society,       156) Tj T*
(including professional men with large incomes, would then escape) Tj T*
(taxation largely or altogether. Income on the surface seems the) Tj T*
(fairest measure of ability, but is objected to because the incomes of) Tj T*
(different individuals, both on account of source and size, really) Tj T*
(indicate unequal and not similar abilities. In practice, however, all) Tj T*
(three methods are employed in all advanced states, so it is not) Tj T*
(necessary to decide which is theoretically the fairest.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Still another practical question confronts us after we adopt the) Tj T*
(ability theory: Shall the rate of taxation be the same no matter what) Tj T*
(the amount of the property or income, or shall it increase as the) Tj T*
(amount grows larger? In other words, shall taxation be proportional or) Tj T*
(progressive? In general the advocates of the ability theory also) Tj T*
(support progression, though there are many exceptions to this) Tj T*
(statement. Three main arguments have been urged in support of this) Tj T*
(method. First, progression is advocated in order to secure equality of) Tj T*
(sacrifice; it is argued that each dollar of a $10,000 income affords) Tj T*
(less gratification to the owner than each dollar of a $1,000 income,) Tj T*
(and that consequently in order to equalize the sacrifices of the two) Tj T*
(individuals a larger proportion of the first income should be taken) Tj T*
(than of the second. Objection is made to this, that wants expand even) Tj T*
(more rapidly than incomes and therefore the initial assumption is) Tj T*
(untrue. Progression is urged, in the second place, by those who desire) Tj T*
(to use taxation as a method of introducing social reforms or of) Tj T*
(bringing about a more equitable distribution of wealth, as by the) Tj T*
(breaking up of large fortunes. It seems inadvisable, however, to use) Tj T*
(the machinery of taxation for such purposes. Other writers urge that) Tj T*
(the ability to earn or produce wealth increases at an accelerating) Tj T*
(rate, and that taxation should therefore keep pace with it. ?It is the) Tj T*
(first thousand that counts.? The objection is made here that it would) Tj T*
(penalize ability and energy. In general, while the arguments are not     157) Tj T*
(conclusive, progression certainly secures a nearer approach to the) Tj T*
(ideal of the ability theory than does proportional taxation. The) Tj T*
(practical application, after we accept it, is still a difficult) Tj T*
(matter. It should be applied to the revenue system as a whole by the) Tj T*
(careful selection of special taxes. As a matter of fact we have just) Tj T*
(the opposite system in the United States, for the poor man undoubtedly) Tj T*
(pays out a larger proportion of his income in taxes--principally on) Tj T*
