(In the main there has been a clear division in the United States) Tj T*
(between the sources of income of the Federal Government on the one) Tj T*
(hand and those of the state and local governments on the other. The) Tj T*
(Federal Treasury has derived its revenue almost entirely from indirect) Tj T*
(taxes--excise and customs--while the other governments have depended) Tj T*
(chiefly upon direct taxes upon persons, property, business,) Tj T*
(corporations, and inheritances. The division rests upon the) Tj T*
(constitutional allotment of powers, but it also corresponds very) Tj T*
(closely to the industrial and political functions of each in their) Tj T*
(relations to the individual citizens. The chief duty of the Federal) Tj T*
(Government is that of national defense and foreign intercourse,) Tj T*
(relations which are national in extent but which affect the individual) Tj T*
(only remotely; so, too, its taxing area is national and its exactions) Tj T*
(are felt only distantly. Few persons, it has been said, taste the tax) Tj T*
(in their tea or their whisky, yet over one-third of all the taxes) Tj T*
(collected in the United States are derived from either customs or) Tj T*
(excise duties. Whisky and tobacco contribute most of the internal) Tj T*
(revenue, while import duties are levied on practically everything) Tj T*
(brought into the country which could compete with any home product.) Tj T*
(These two sources yield over $500,000,000 a year to the Federal) Tj T*
(Treasury. During the Civil War these sources of revenue were) Tj T*
(supplemented by a federal income tax, but as such a tax was later) Tj T*
(declared unconstitutional by the Supreme Court, further recourse to      158) Tj T*
(this in the near future seems improbable. From a purely economic and) Tj T*
(financial point of view this is very regrettable, for the Federal) Tj T*
(Government should unquestionably have at its command the means of) Tj T*
(quickly raising large additional revenue with as little disturbance to) Tj T*
(industry as possible. Such a means would be afforded by the income) Tj T*
(tax, which moreover can be administered only by the Federal) Tj T*
(Government, as it must be national in its operations to be fair.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The main reliance of the state and local governments in this country) Tj T*
(is the general property tax, which amounted in 1902 to over) Tj T*
($700,000,000 or almost half of all the taxes collected. This really) Tj T*
(consists of two very distinct parts, which present quite different) Tj T*
(problems, namely, the tax on real property and that on personal) Tj T*
(property. Under our peculiar system, by which property is assessed) Tj T*
(locally, and upon the basis of that assessment its share of the state) Tj T*
(taxes distributed to each locality, there is every incentive offered) Tj T*
(to the local assessor to under-value the land in his jurisdiction,) Tj T*
(thereby escaping part of the state burdens. This evil of inequality) Tj T*
(between localities could be obviated by the simple expedient of) Tj T*
(relieving real estate of all state taxes and leaving it solely to the) Tj T*
(counties and cities for purposes of taxation. In the case of personal) Tj T*
(property the great evil is evasion. Much of our modern wealth exists) Tj T*
(in the form of securities, stocks, bonds, mortgages, etc., and this is) Tj T*
(practically undiscoverable by assessors except by the voluntary) Tj T*
(declaration of the tax-payer, which is only truthfully made by) Tj T*
(trustees and a few conscientious persons. Most of our laws have been) Tj T*
(directed to the discovery of this intangible property, as it is) Tj T*
