(prevailed in the United States for virtually one hundred years, and) Tj T*
(could not be suddenly changed and abolished if one would. From the) Tj T*
(financial standpoint, too, import duties are absolutely essential to) Tj T*
(the support of our Federal Government; the question here is not) Tj T*
(absolutely free trade, but the choice of articles for revenue) Tj T*
(purposes. Shall they be those which are not produced in this country) Tj T*
(or those which enter into competition with domestic products? If) Tj T*
(financial considerations alone prevailed, the former would undoubtedly) Tj T*
(be selected as the more convenient, certain, and economical. But in) Tj T*
(the determination of the tariff policies of the United States economic) Tj T*
(considerations have been paramount and to an examination of these we) Tj T*
(must now turn.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Historically the following arguments have played the main role in) Tj T*
(support of protection at different times in the United States. The) Tj T*
(infant industries argument was advanced by Hamilton in his celebrated) Tj T*
(Report on Manufactures in 1791 and has always been important until) Tj T*
(recently when the infants had grown to be so lusty that it was evident) Tj T*
(that other reasons for protecting them must be discovered. This was      161) Tj T*
(found in the plea for diversified production, which was necessary for) Tj T*
(a well-rounded economic development; the need of creating a strong) Tj T*
(national government and national spirit also played their part. In) Tj T*
(order to win over the farmers the home market argument was early) Tj T*
(urged; this has taken various forms. In the first place it was urged) Tj T*
(that the building up of manufacturing centers and the consequent) Tj T*
(increase in population would give the farmers a better market than the) Tj T*
(fluctuating foreign one. As set forth by Carey, it would keep within) Tj T*
(the country the elements taken from the soil. It would also save the) Tj T*
(freights on the transportation of goods back and forth across the) Tj T*
(ocean. Each of these arguments has lost force with the development of) Tj T*
(the country and the decrease in the cost of transportation. More) Tj T*
(important today is the wages argument; at first protection was urged) Tj T*
(because wages were high in the United States and the manufacturer) Tj T*
(needed to be protected against his foreign competitor who employed) Tj T*
(cheap labor. Today it is argued that protection has raised wages and) Tj T*
(must be continued in order to protect the laborer against the pauper) Tj T*
(labor of Europe. Curiously enough, in France protection is urged for) Tj T*
(French workmen against the highly paid and efficient American. The) Tj T*
(effect of the tariff on wages has been greatly exaggerated; wages are) Tj T*
(high in the United States because the productivity of labor is high.) Tj T*
(Indeed so far as the tariff raises prices it may be argued that the) Tj T*
(real wages of labor are lowered. More generally accepted as defensible) Tj T*
(grounds for protection are the political arguments that a nation) Tj T*
(should be able to produce its own military armaments and supplies, and) Tj T*
(that it should be able to use the tariff as a retaliatory measure.) Tj T*
(Recently this latter has received considerable force from the practice) Tj T*
(of ?dumping,? by which is meant the occasional sale of products abroad) Tj T*
(at prices lower than those charged at home. Domestic manufacturers in) Tj T*
(the country thus treated are of course seriously injured and have        162) Tj T*
(insisted upon protection against this procedure which has been) Tj T*
(authorized in Canada.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
