(In answer to these various arguments the free traders, or those) Tj T*
(desiring a modification of present high rates, make their main appeal) Tj T*
(to the doctrine of comparative costs. Briefly stated this asserts that) Tj T*
(nations, like individuals, can do some things better than others. Like) Tj T*
(the individual lawyer therefore who pays to have his boots blacked) Tj T*
(while he devotes himself to the law, the nation should produce the) Tj T*
(things it is best fitted for and pay others to produce other things) Tj T*
(which it can do less well. In this way each will obtain the largest) Tj T*
(possible return. Protection, which interferes with this natural) Tj T*
(international division of labor, simply diverts labor and capital from) Tj T*
(more into less profitable industries. Practically, this purely) Tj T*
(abstract economic argument has had little influence on the commercial) Tj T*
(policy of nations, which have been moved more by political and) Tj T*
(industrial considerations. Today, however, there is no question but) Tj T*
(that the freer movement of capital and industry throughout the world) Tj T*
(would be advantageous. In answer to the home market argument it is) Tj T*
(pointed out that with the growth of large-scale production the) Tj T*
(profitable area of manufacture has greatly widened and now in many) Tj T*
(cases transcends national boundaries. As home producers seek foreign) Tj T*
(markets, as they are beginning to do, they themselves will demand a) Tj T*
(reduction of the tariff, especially in the matter of raw materials.) Tj T*
(Free traders also deny the need of artificially diversifying industry) Tj T*
(in a country as large and varied as the United States, or of building) Tj T*
(up infant industries. Indeed, on the latter point, they urge that many) Tj T*
(of our trusts are the result of the tariff, and that the attempt to) Tj T*
(grant legislative favors has resulted only in wholesale demoralization) Tj T*
(and a debauching of our national politics.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In conclusion it may be said that under certain conditions the           163) Tj T*
(policy of protection is relatively defensible; that it has undoubtedly) Tj T*
(hastened the industrial development of the United States, though it) Tj T*
(has not caused it; and that, on the other hand, it is responsible for) Tj T*
(not a few evils in our political and industrial life. The struggle of) Tj T*
(particular interests during the framing of the Payne bill shows the) Tj T*
(impossibility of deciding this issue upon academic grounds. It may be) Tj T*
(prophesied, however, that as our manufacturers reach out more) Tj T*
(seriously after the foreign markets the tariff will be modified so as) Tj T*
(to make this possible; but he would be a rash prophet who should) Tj T*
(predict a sudden or great change in our tariff policy within the) Tj T*
(present generation.) Tj T*
