(XIX. ECONOMIC PROGRESS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At the conclusion of a study of this character we are inevitably led) Tj T*
(to summarize our conclusions and to try to answer the question as to) Tj T*
(what the lessons of the past have taught us. In what direction are the) Tj T*
(forces of economic life taking us? The conclusion of this text is that) Tj T*
(they are making for economic progress, and it will be worth while to) Tj T*
(justify as far as possible this belief. It is, however, impossible       173) Tj T*
(to do this except in very general terms, for definite data for) Tj T*
(measuring this improvement do not exist, and economic progress itself) Tj T*
(is a somewhat vague conception. Even such comparatively simple facts) Tj T*
(as the rate of wages or the hours of labor can be stated only very) Tj T*
(generally. But both of these show a decided improvement in the) Tj T*
(condition of the working class. A careful investigation for Great) Tj T*
(Britain by Mr. A. L. Bowley[50] shows that if wages for the decade) Tj T*
