(wages started at a higher level. According to the Aldrich report, if) Tj T*
(wages and prices in 1860 in the United States be taken as 100,) Tj T*
(relative wages in 1840 were 82.5 and relative prices 98.5; in 1880,) Tj T*
(they were respectively 143 and 103.4; in 1903, they were 187 and 103.) Tj T*
(That is to say, relative wages showed a marked advance and real wages,) Tj T*
(owing to the fact that general prices remained almost stationary, an) Tj T*
(even greater improvement. So, too, the hours of labor appear to have) Tj T*
(been shortened in Great Britain about two hours a day \(from 10 to 14) Tj T*
(hours to 8 to 12\), and in the United States probably as much, the) Tj T*
(average length of the working day in certain employments decreasing      174) Tj T*
(from 10.3 hours in 1880 to 9.6 hours in 1903.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
