(CONCRETE AND STEEL.                                                      322) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(BY J. F. SPRINGER.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([New York author of articles in Applied Science.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The life of properly made concrete is not known. However, specimens) Tj T*
(from the times of the Romans are yet in good condition. This material) Tj T*
(has very considerable ability to resist compression; it is practically) Tj T*
(fireproof and teredo proof; when properly protected it is probably but) Tj T*
(little deteriorated by weather changes; and, if properly made, it is) Tj T*
(probably inappreciably subject to chemical disintegration when) Tj T*
(submerged. But there is one palpable fault--it is weak when subjected) Tj T*
(to tensile stresses. On the other hand--steel has great tensile) Tj T*
(resistivity and is strong under transverse stress. These two) Tj T*
(materials--concrete and steel--supplement each other in valuable) Tj T*
(qualities. The possibility of using them in combination depends) Tj T*
(largely upon the fact that their co-efficients of expansion are) Tj T*
(practically the same for moderate thermal fluctuations. Steel is) Tj T*
(easily corroded. Nor is it strictly fireproof, as temperatures which) Tj T*
(are not excessive will induce bending and buckling. When it is) Tj T*
(surrounded by concrete, steel is protected against both fire and) Tj T*
(corrosion.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In many situations, steel would not alone supply the best material of) Tj T*
(construction. And the same remark applies to concrete. A striking) Tj T*
(instance is the case of the six new docks in Baltimore. Three of these) Tj T*
(had already been constructed of wood and stone, when it became evident) Tj T*
(that the building of the remaining three and of the long bulkhead) Tj T*
(which was part of the scheme along the same lines would entail a) Tj T*
(larger expense than the use of reinforced concrete construction. Steel) Tj T*
(by itself would have been impossible of consideration because of its) Tj T*
(susceptibility to corrosion. Concrete alone could not be used because    323) Tj T*
