(The story of soda is a beautiful example of how industry and the need) Tj T*
(of civilization can act as a beacon light for the science of             348) Tj T*
(chemistry. This illustration will show how the pure science has) Tj T*
(created new industries and opened up new activities for civilization.) Tj T*
(In 1838 in England, there was born a boy who afterwards was to be) Tj T*
(known as Sir Wm. Perkin. He came of a very intelligent family.) Tj T*
(Besides, he was gifted with a natural aptitude for chemistry. More) Tj T*
(than that, he was put under the direction of Professor Hofmann, one of) Tj T*
(the most brilliant of chemists. Perkin would have been called by any) Tj T*
(one, an ideal bit of raw material. Hofmann, like many others of those) Tj T*
(German chemists, had a faculty of instilling that enthusiasm that is) Tj T*
(necessary in the performance of an epoch-making advancement. Perkin) Tj T*
(caught that enthusiasm. He rigged up a laboratory in his house and) Tj T*
(worked at night and in his vacations on those interesting problems) Tj T*
(that Hofmann discussed in his lectures. During one of these vacations,) Tj T*
(he was trying to build up, artificially, the substance called quinine,) Tj T*
(which was up to that time a purely natural product. His work took an) Tj T*
(unexpected turn. Instead of building up quinine, he built what) Tj T*
(chemists call now phenyl-sufranine, or mauvÎine. This was a new) Tj T*
(substance with properties that rendered it an excellent dye. Perkin) Tj T*
(established a factory in which the new substance could be prepared on) Tj T*
(a large scale; and within a year of its discovery, he had it on the) Tj T*
(market. This discovery of Mauve, the first of the artificial dyes,) Tj T*
(gave a great impetus to the study of coal tar, from which it was made.) Tj T*
(Coal tar, up to that time, was a waste product, made in the process of) Tj T*
(heating coal for the manufacture of gas. This coal tar is the raw) Tj T*
(material which is used in that enormous chemical industry, the) Tj T*
(manufacture of the derivatives of tri-phenyl methane, the so-called) Tj T*
(aniline dyes. There is invested in this industry alone, $750,000,000;) Tj T*
(and the whole structure, complex as it is, is built on the foundation) Tj T*
(of a pure chemical research that was undertaken merely to gratify the) Tj T*
(investigative desires of a true scientist, with no thought of its        349) Tj T*
(financial results. This achievement of Perkin stands out as one of the) Tj T*
(great discoveries of chemistry. And the story of Mauve shows how) Tj T*
(science has led the way for industry, just as the story of soda shows) Tj T*
(how industry has pointed out the way for science.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Many more stories of the victories of scientific industry could be) Tj T*
(told. Much has been done. But the chemist does not live in the glory) Tj T*
(of the past. He lives in the possibilities of the future. Every) Tj T*
(advancement of the past has opened up many fields of possibilities. If) Tj T*
(much has been done, much more remains to be done. And the work of the) Tj T*
(future will require the services of the scientist more than did the) Tj T*
(work of the past. Those problems whose answers were obvious, have all) Tj T*
(been solved. The problems of today are deep ones; they require all the) Tj T*
(ingenuity, all the ability that the trained chemist can bring to bear) Tj T*
(upon the problems. And they will all tend to increased efficiency.) Tj T*
