(In the most fundamental terms, chemistry concerns itself with the) Tj T*
(changes which the different kinds of energy produce upon matter.         350) Tj T*
(Chemistry concerns itself with two things, energy and matter. And yet) Tj T*
(chemistry must admit that it does not know the nature of matter or the) Tj T*
(nature of energy. And not knowing, it cannot appreciate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In this direction lie the achievements of the chemistry of the future.) Tj T*
(As the nature of matter and the nature of energy gradually unfold) Tj T*
(themselves to the advances of chemical investigation, remarkable) Tj T*
(possibilities for future development are disclosed. We are beginning) Tj T*
(to see how really wasteful we have been. The frightful wastes which) Tj T*
(the movement toward the conservation of our natural resources has) Tj T*
(called to our attention, sink into utter insignificance when we) Tj T*
(consider what we have lost on account of our ignorance. We are just) Tj T*
(beginning to appreciate our wastefulness of chemical energy. A piece) Tj T*
(of coal, for example, has in it the possibility of doing ten times as) Tj T*
(much work as it is doing now. A piece of radium has stored in it an) Tj T*
(almost infinite amount of energy. How to change this internal or) Tj T*
(chemical energy into the other forms of energy with which we are) Tj T*
(familiar, into heat, or electricity, or ordinary mechanical energy,) Tj T*
(that is the problem of the future. The utilization of this vast amount) Tj T*
(of potential energy that is stored up in all forms of matter, the) Tj T*
(harnessing of it in the service of humanity, this is the problem which) Tj T*
(confronts the chemist. It goes down to the very fundamentals of his) Tj T*
(science.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But the start has been made. The point of the wedge has already found) Tj T*
(entrance. The discovery of radium, and the study of its) Tj T*
(decompositions, has opened wide our field of vision. The problem must) Tj T*
(yield, as the blows of chemical investigation fall upon the wedge and) Tj T*
(drive it home.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Chemistry has always been a utilitarian science. Its results have) Tj T*
(always been at the service of humanity. And if we can judge the future) Tj T*
(by the past, even discounting for the enthusiasm of the chemist, we) Tj T*
(can forsee improved processes which will reduce our present wasteful     351) Tj T*
(methods; we can see new processes making for us such things as india) Tj T*
(rubber from starch, for which we must now depend upon the bounty of) Tj T*
(nature; and we can dimly see the time when we shall be able to utilize) Tj T*
