(Official reports show that the coal placed on the market amounts) Tj T*
(annually to between 450,000,000 and 500,000,000 short tons in the) Tj T*
(United States alone. These figures, however, are somewhat misleading) Tj T*
(as they do not in any way show the tremendous wastes that are going on) Tj T*
(due to our present methods of mining and restrictions in qualities of) Tj T*
(coal that can be transported and placed on the market at a reasonable) Tj T*
(profit. Careful investigation has shown that the coal wasted or left) Tj T*
(in the mines in such form as to be inaccessible to future generations) Tj T*
(amounts each year to practically 100 per cent of that placed on the) Tj T*
(market, or in other words, at the present time some 450,000,000 tons) Tj T*
(are annually lost as far as commercial value is concerned.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If this condition is allowed to continue it is estimated by the United) Tj T*
(States Geological Survey that our available supply of bituminous coal) Tj T*
(will be exhausted within the next two hundred years.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A realization of the seriousness of this situation has led to a) Tj T*
(careful and systematic study of the present lack of efficiency in the) Tj T*
(utilization of fuels for both power and metallurgical purposes, to) Tj T*
(investigations into more efficient use of the present marketable) Tj T*
(grades of fuel, and to a consideration of methods of using the) Tj T*
(so-called low-grade fuels, lignites and peats.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The United States Geological Survey has for several years been) Tj T*
(investigating the economic value of coals and lignites as gas-producer) Tj T*
(fuel. This work, begun with tests of coal and lignite at the             353) Tj T*
(coal-testing plant erected at the Louisiana Purchase Exposition, St.) Tj T*
(Louis, Mo., in 1904, was continued at St. Louis and at Norfolk, Va.,) Tj T*
(and is now being carried on by the Survey at the fuel-testing plant in) Tj T*
(Pittsburg, Pa. The tests were undertaken because it was evidently) Tj T*
(desirable to determine the value of the gas producer as a means of) Tj T*
(increasing efficiency in the use of the coal supplies of the United) Tj T*
(States. The early tests proved decidedly encouraging, demonstrating) Tj T*
(that many coals now wasted or not mined because they are not) Tj T*
(satisfactory fuel for steam-power plants can, by conversion into) Tj T*
(producer-gas, be made to do from two to three times as much work as) Tj T*
(can be done by the best grades of steam coal burned in a boiler plant.) Tj T*
