(Rapid Development of the Gas Engine.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was not until late in the nineteenth century that the gas engine) Tj T*
(came into common use, and although many types have been devised within) Tj T*
(the last twenty or thirty years it is only within eight or nine years) Tj T*
(that large gas engines have been constructed. This development started) Tj T*
(eleven or twelve years ago in Germany, Belgium, and England, but) Tj T*
(marked progress has been limited to the last eight years.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For a long time the natural fuel of these internal-combustion engines) Tj T*
(was city gas, but this was too expensive except for engines of small) Tj T*
(capacity. It was seldom found economical to operate units of more than) Tj T*
(75 horsepower with this fuel. Cheap gas was essential for the) Tj T*
(development of the gas engine, but the early attempts to produce cheap) Tj T*
(gas were somewhat discouraging, and for a time it seemed very            354) Tj T*
(unlikely that the gas engine would encroach to any extent on the field) Tj T*
(occupied by the steam engine. The theoretical possibilities of the) Tj T*
(internal-combustion engine operating with cheap fuel promised so much,) Tj T*
(however, that the practical difficulties were rapidly overcome, with) Tj T*
(the result that the internal-combustion engine has become a serious) Tj T*
(rival of the steam engine in many of its applications.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The development of the large gas engine within the last few years has) Tj T*
(been exceedingly rapid. It was only ten years ago that a) Tj T*
(600-horsepower engine exhibited at the Paris Exposition was regarded) Tj T*
(as a wonder, but today four-cycle, twin-tandem, double-acting engines) Tj T*
(of 2,000 to 3,500 horsepower can be found in nearly all up-to-date) Tj T*
(steel plants, and there are installations in this country containing) Tj T*
(several units rated at 5,400 horsepower each.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Development of the Gas Producer for Power Purposes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The rapid advance of the large gas engine was made possible by) Tj T*
(improvements in the production of cheap gas directly from fuel by) Tj T*
(means of the gas producer. An early form of producer introduced in) Tj T*
(Europe, and now in general use both abroad and in the United States,) Tj T*
(is known as the suction producer, a name suggested by the fact that) Tj T*
(the engine develops its charge of gas in the producer by means of its) Tj T*
(own suction stroke. Although many producers of this type are now used,) Tj T*
(most of them are small, seldom exceeding 200 horsepower. A serious) Tj T*
(limitation to the utility of the suction producer has been the fact) Tj T*
(that, owing to the manner of generating the gas, no tarry fuels could) Tj T*
(be used, a restriction that prevented the use of bituminous coals,) Tj T*
(lignites, peats, and other like fuels. The fuels in most common use) Tj T*
(for producers of this type are charcoal, coke, and anthracite coal,) Tj T*
