([Consulting Efficiency Engineer, New York. Published in The Iron Age,) Tj T*
(Jan. 6, 1910, and reproduced by special arrangement.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Managers of industrial enterprises will undoubtedly agree that there) Tj T*
(are few qualities which are more to be desired in equipment, methods) Tj T*
(and men than that of efficiency. From an extensive study of this) Tj T*
(subject in various parts of the country, together with interviews and) Tj T*
(correspondence with several hundred concerns, the writer has become) Tj T*
(convinced that there is a general lack of definite comprehension of) Tj T*
(what efficiency is, whence it springs, how it may be measured and) Tj T*
(developed and the results which its cultivation will produce. The) Tj T*
(object of this monograph is an endeavor to throw some light upon these) Tj T*
(things and to afford a new viewpoint from which to study industrial) Tj T*
(operations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Evolution of Industrial Management.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the first place we must realize that the management of industrial) Tj T*
(enterprises is in a state of evolution. The tremendous growth of the) Tj T*
(past few years has caused certain previously satisfactory methods to) Tj T*
(become inadequate to present needs. Many details which in the days of) Tj T*
(smaller affairs could be absorbed by personal inspection and mentally) Tj T*
(stored for use when needed must now, because of their very volume, be) Tj T*
(made matters of record.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The character of these records has much to do with their value.) Tj T*
(Because financial records are so ancient they have exerted an undue) Tj T*
(influence upon the character of all other records. While under our) Tj T*
(present civilization, the ultimate object of industrial operations is) Tj T*
(to create financial profits, there are many highly important records) Tj T*
(which cannot be adequately expressed in terms of money. The business     371) Tj T*
(of manufacturing consists of a repetition of mechanical operations.) Tj T*
(Mechanical operations necessarily involve considerations of weight,) Tj T*
(distance, time and effort, but not of money.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The reason for the failure of so many cost systems to serve the) Tj T*
(desired end is that they are based upon a wrong unit. These systems) Tj T*
(become useful only beyond a certain point. Other systems have been the) Tj T*
(result of a blind craving for aid, but being without broad underlying) Tj T*
(principles and not properly tied together and simply, in many cases,) Tj T*
(disjointed attempts to improve isolated details, they too have failed.) Tj T*
(The result is that attempts by specialists to improve industrial) Tj T*
