(conditions have been often looked upon with suspicion and this is not) Tj T*
(altogether without reason. These very failures, however, have drawn) Tj T*
(the attention of men in certain lines of engineering to the rapidly) Tj T*
(developing needs of manufacturers. They have attempted to solve the) Tj T*
(problems by the use of engineering instead of by accounting methods,) Tj T*
(and the results which have been attained prove conclusively that a) Tj T*
(material advance has been made.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(What Is Efficiency?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With this understanding of the present conditions, let us consider) Tj T*
(what efficiency really is. It has been defined as ?the ability to) Tj T*
(produce certain results,? and this at the very outset necessitates the) Tj T*
(existence or creation of a standard of measurement. Our perception of) Tj T*
(efficiency, therefore, is correct only in proportion to the precision) Tj T*
(of the standard, which must be accurately developed from data which) Tj T*
(are not only exact, but complete. A machinist, believed to be) Tj T*
(operating at high efficiency, was observed while turning a shaft. His) Tj T*
(cut, feed and speed seemed to be beyond criticism. When the shaft was) Tj T*
(finished, however, he had to spend half as much time in hunting up a) Tj T*
(chain and pad to remove the shaft from the lathe, as he had taken in     372) Tj T*
(turning it. This cut his actual efficiency from 100 per cent down to) Tj T*
(87 per cent, yet the man was not at fault. His normal work was to) Tj T*
(operate a lathe and not to hunt for things which should have been) Tj T*
(provided for him. The points to be observed here are not only the) Tj T*
(importance of using the right standard of measurement, but that the) Tj T*
(efficiency of the man depended very largely upon his surrounding) Tj T*
(conditions over which he had no control. These conditions depend upon) Tj T*
(the efficiency of the management in securing proper equipment from the) Tj T*
(owners. This in turn depends upon the efficiency of the management?s) Tj T*
(records in enabling it to state clearly and accurately what increase) Tj T*
(in output and consequently in profits will result from improving the) Tj T*
(conditions--thus justifying the expenditure required. We see from this) Tj T*
(that the true standard is not the possibility under existing) Tj T*
(conditions, but that which can be obtained under other and more) Tj T*
(desirable conditions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Managerial Opposition to Change.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The management, which immediately controls the records and conditions) Tj T*
(should be the prime source of efforts towards the increase of) Tj T*
(efficiency throughout the plant. The opposition of managers to) Tj T*
(progress in this respect is exceedingly great, yet not altogether) Tj T*
(surprising for these reasons:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. There is a widespread fallacy that so-called practical experience) Tj T*
(in the manual operations or technical processes of a business is the) Tj T*
(chief essential to success in its management. This is due to the fact) Tj T*
(that perfection of workmanship, of which he knows much, is more) Tj T*
(important in the eyes of the artisan than the actual cost of the) Tj T*
