(THE BRIDGE BETWEEN LABOR AND CAPITAL.                                    380) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(BY JOHN MITCHELL.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Former President United Mine Workers of America.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If the interests of labor and capital were identical--as some) Tj T*
(contend--there would be no chasm between them to bridge; and if the) Tj T*
(interests of labor and capital were irreconcilable--as others) Tj T*
(contend--any effort to unite them would be futile. From an experience) Tj T*
(extending over a considerable period, I am quite convinced that) Tj T*
(neither of the foregoing propositions will stand the test of close) Tj T*
(analysis. My judgment is that the interests of labor and capital,) Tj T*
(though divergent in some respects, are nevertheless reciprocal and) Tj T*
(inter-dependent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
